# Local development environment for `make run`.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   make run
#
# Copy it, do not rename it — .env is gitignored and .env.example is what
# belongs in git. `make run` sources the copy, so this is shell syntax: no
# spaces around `=`, and quote any value containing a space.
#
# Every setting the brain reads has a default in internal/config, and those
# defaults already match the ports deployment/docker-compose.yml publishes.
# So the only thing that has to be written down here is authentication —
# without it the brain refuses to start, which is the whole reason a bare
# `go run ./cmd/brain` exits 1 on a fresh clone.

# A shared token, accepted only in development. Send it as a bearer token:
#   curl -H "Authorization: Bearer letmein" 127.0.0.1:21120/whoami
OPENARITY_ENVIRONMENT=development
OPENARITY_DEV_TOKEN=letmein

# Who the dev token authenticates as, and who may create teams. The dev token
# has no identity provider behind it, so this is the subject it claims.
OPENARITY_SUPER_ADMINS=dev

# Uncomment to run against a real identity provider instead. The dev token
# must be empty then — the brain will not start with both, and outside
# development it is refused outright. See deployment/QUICKSTART.md.
#
# OPENARITY_DEV_TOKEN=
# OPENARITY_OIDC_ENABLED=true
# OPENARITY_OIDC_ISSUER=http://localhost:9000/application/o/openarity/
# OPENARITY_OIDC_AUDIENCE=<the provider's client ID>
# OPENARITY_SUPER_ADMINS=akadmin

# Needed if POSTGRES_PORT in deployment/.env was moved off 5432, and on any
# machine already running its own Postgres — the default points at
# localhost:5432, which is then someone else's server without our database.
#
# OPENARITY_POSTGRES_DSN=postgres://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:15432/openarity?sslmode=disable
# OPENARITY_LOG_LEVEL=debug

# Whichever database this ends up pointing at needs the schema once:
#
#   set -a; . ./.env; set +a; go run ./cmd/brain migrate up
#
# Skipping it starts a healthy server on which every route returns 500.
